      "lem:regular-tail-wellposedness"
    ],
    "justification": "This identifies the complete-randomization initialization and the exact causal decomposition that the stopped process must transport.",
    "gap": "Li and Ding give the finite-population vector CLT at a fixed complete-randomization draw, not along the Zhu--Liu first-entry path.",
    "consumer": "The joint normalization supplies the initial law for the stopped martingale problem and the ATE scaling used by every later interval result.",
    "proof_tex": "The score definition and covariate centering give\n\\[\n \\sum_{i=1}^n d_{ni}=0,\\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^n r_{ni}=0.\n\\]\nThe projection normal equations give \\(S_{dr,n}=0\\).  Moreover,\n\\[\n S_{dd,n}=S_{xx,n}^{-1/2}S_{xx,n}S_{xx,n}^{-1/2}=I_k,\n \\qquad S_{rr,n}=s_n^{-2}v_n=1.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n \\frac1{n-1}\\sum_{i=1}^n q_{ni}q_{ni}^{\\mathsf T}=I_{k+1}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\nUniform boundedness, the lower eigenvalue bound, and \\(s_n\\ge\\sqrt{c_s/2}\\) imply \\(\\max_i\\|q_{ni}\\|\\le C\\) on the retained tail for a deterministic \\(C<\\infty\\).  In particular \\(n^{-1}\\max_i\\|q_{ni}\\|^2\\to0\\).  The complete-randomization vector central limit lemma applied to (1) now yields\n\\[\n Z_n(W_{n,0})=n^{-1/2}\\sum_{i=1}^n(2W_{n,0,i}-1)q_{ni}\n \\Rightarrow \\mathcal N_{k+1}(0,I_{k+1}).\n\\]\nFor reference, the covariance is exactly the identity: balanced sampling gives \\(\\operatorname{Cov}(2W_i-1,2W_j-1)=-1/(n-1)\\) for \\(i\\ne j\\), and centering plus (1) gives \\(\\operatorname{Var}\\{Z_n(W_{n,0})\\}=I_{k+1}\\).\n\nFor the causal identity, fixed potential outcomes and equal allocation imply, for every \\(w\\in\\mathcal A_n\\),\n\\[\n \\widehat\\tau_n(w)-\\tau_n\n =\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n(2w_i-1)\\{Y_{ni}(1)+Y_{ni}(0)\\}\n =\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n(2w_i-1)(h_{ni}-\\bar h_n),\n\\]\nwhere the last equality uses \\(\\sum_i(2w_i-1)=0\\).  The score construction gives the exact projection identity\n\\[\n h_{ni}-\\bar h_n=b_n^{\\mathsf T}d_{ni}+s_nr_{ni}.\n\\]\nSubstitution, followed by taking \\(w=W_{n,\\mathrm{stop}}\\), proves\n\\[\n \\sqrt n(\\widehat\\tau_n-\\tau_n)\n =b_n^{\\mathsf T}D_n(W_{n,\\mathrm{stop}})+s_nE_n(W_{n,\\mathrm{stop}})\n\\]\nwithout an approximation or a superpopulation argument.",
